latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Create a deep copy of the input grid to modify.\n\n2. Define a function to identify a 3x3 blue square pattern:\n   - Check if the corners and center are blue (1)\n   - Check if the middle of each side is black (0)\n\n3. Define a function to transform a 3x3 pattern to sky blue:\n   - Change the corners and center to sky blue (8)\n\n4. Iterate through the grid row by row:\n   a. Initialize an empty list to store the positions of blue patterns in the current row.\n   \n   b. Scan the current row to find all 3x3 blue patterns:\n      - For each column in the row (up to width - 2):\n        * If a blue pattern is found, add its starting column to the list.\n\n   c. If the list of pattern positions is not empty:\n      - Keep the leftmost pattern blue (skip the first position in the list).\n      - For all other positions in the list:\n        * Transform the pattern at that position to sky blue.\n\n   d. After processing existing patterns, add new sky blue patterns:\n      - Calculate the space between existing patterns.\n      - While there's enough space in the row:\n        * Add a new sky blue pattern maintaining the same spacing.\n\n5. Return the modified grid.\n\nDetailed algorithm:\n\n```\nfunction solve_5b526a93(input_grid):\n    grid = deep_copy(input_grid)\n    rows, cols = grid.get_dimensions()\n\n    function is_blue_pattern(row, col):\n        pattern = [[1, 0, 1], [0, 1, 0], [1, 0, 1]]\n        for i in range(3):\n            for j in range(3):\n                if grid.get_cell(row + i, col + j) != pattern[i][j]:\n                    return False\n        return True\n\n    function transform_to_sky_blue(row, col):\n        for i in [0, 2]:\n            for j in [0, 2]:\n                grid.set_cell(row + i, col + j, 8)\n        grid.set_cell(row + 1, col + 1, 8)\n\n    function add_sky_blue_pattern(row, col):\n        transform_to_sky_blue(row, col)\n\n    for row in range(rows - 2):\n        pattern_positions = []\n        for col in range(cols - 2):\n            if is_blue_pattern(row, col):\n                pattern_positions.append(col)\n\n        if pattern_positions:\n            # Skip the first (leftmost) pattern\n            for col in pattern_positions[1:]:\n                transform_to_sky_blue(row, col)\n\n            # Add new patterns\n            last_col = pattern_positions[-1]\n            pattern_width = 3\n            space_between = pattern_positions[1] - pattern_positions[0] if len(pattern_positions) > 1 else pattern_width + 1\n\n            next_col = last_col + space_between\n            while next_col + pattern_width <= cols:\n                if all(grid.get_cell(row + i, next_col + j) == 0 for i in range(3) for j in range(3)):\n                    add_sky_blue_pattern(row, next_col)\n                next_col += space_between\n\n    return grid\n```\n\nThis algorithm follows the revised understanding of the challenge, processing the grid row by row, maintaining the leftmost blue pattern in each row, transforming other existing patterns to sky blue, and adding new sky blue patterns where space allows. It should work consistently across all provided examples and test cases."
